A fitted object of class \code{ctStanFit} (\code{backend='stan'}) or
\code{ctJuliaFit} (\code{backend='julia'}), both also of class \code{ctFit}.
Every fit includes \code{$args}, a list with two parts that are the same on
both backends:
\describe{
 \item{\code{input}}{The arguments passed to \code{ctFit()}, with the
 defaults for those not supplied, so \code{intoverpop} may be
 \code{'auto'}.}
 \item{\code{resolved}}{The settings the fit ran with, after defaults and
 \code{'auto'} were resolved and deprecated arguments merged (such as
 \code{nopriors} into \code{priors}). \code{cores} is a whole number,
 \code{intoverpop} is \code{'augmented'}, \code{'laplace'} or
 \code{'none'}, and \code{priors} is \code{TRUE}, \code{FALSE} or
 \code{'randomCorr'}.}
}
}
\description{
Fits a ctsem model specified via \code{\link{ctModel}} with type either 'ct' or 'dt'.
\code{ctStanFit} is maintained as a backward-compatible alias.
}
\details{
For a julia fit with random effects, \code{fit$identifiability$effects}
reports how much of each random effect each subject's data determine, and
how many subjects' worth of information each population standard deviation
rests on. An effect resting on fewer than two is named in a message, in
\code{summary()} and in \code{\link{ctReport}}, with a suggestion to lower
\code{poprank} or set \code{indvarying = FALSE}.
}
\examples{
\donttest{
# The default backend is julia, which ctJuliaInstall() sets up.
if (isTRUE(ctJuliaStatus()$available)) {

# Long format data: one row per subject and occasion, with an id, a time,
# the measured variables Y1 and Y2, and a time dependent predictor TD1.
print(head(ctsemtestdat))

# Two processes, each measured by one variable, affected by TD1. Every
# other part of the model takes the defaults of ctModel().
model <- ctModel(LAMBDA = diag(2),
  manifestNames = c('Y1', 'Y2'), latentNames = c('eta1', 'eta2'),
  TDpredNames = 'TD1')

# priors = TRUE helps with a data set this small (30 subjects).
fit <- ctFit(ctsemtestdat, model, priors = TRUE)
print(summary(fit))

# The effects over time intervals, and predictions for two subjects.
print(ctDiscretePars(fit, times = seq(0, 5, by = 0.1), plot = TRUE))
print(ctPredict(fit, subjects = 1:2, plot = TRUE))

# A linear growth curve for Y1: no dynamics and no process noise, with a
# slope (CINT) and an initial level (T0MEANS) that vary between subjects.
growth <- ctModel(LAMBDA = matrix(1),
  manifestNames = 'Y1', latentNames = 'level',
  DRIFT = 0, DIFFUSION = 0, T0VAR = 0, MANIFESTMEANS = 0,
  CINT = 'slope', T0MEANS = 'initial')
fitgrowth <- ctFit(ctsemtestdat, growth, priors = TRUE)
print(summary(fitgrowth)$popmeans)
summary(fitgrowth)$popsd

# Sampling the posterior instead of optimising:
# ctFit(ctsemtestdat, model, optimize = FALSE)
# or, after a fit, ctFitUncertainty(fit, 'sample').
}
}
}
\seealso{
\code{\link{ctOptimControl}} for the optimiser settings,
\code{\link{ctFitUncertainty}} for uncertainty after a fit,
\code{\link{ctIdentify}} to check which parameters the data can inform
before fitting, and \code{\link{ctTracePlot}} to plot the optimisation trace
of a julia fit.
}
